Relevant columns, their usage, and the main filters we are going to need  

• `event_date` : filter to 20201130 (‑‑ the day of interest)  
• `event_name` : filter to ‘purchase’ events only  
• `ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd` : numerator for the tax‑rate calculation  
• `ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd` : denominator for the tax‑rate calculation  
• `ecommerce.purchase_revenue` : show along with the USD value  
• `ecommerce.total_item_quantity` : requested in the result set  
• `ecommerce.transaction_id` : requested in the result set  
• `items` (REPEATED RECORD)  
  – `item_category` : the “item category” we have to rank by  

Main conditions that will be reused  

```sql
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
```

Below are ten exploratory → final BigQuery queries.  
Every query is limited to at most 20 rows so it is safe to run.

```sql
-- 1. Distinct event names on 30‑Nov‑2020 just to see what we have
SELECT DISTINCT event_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at purchase‑specific ecommerce monetary fields
SELECT
  ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd,
  ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd,
  ecommerce.purchase_revenue
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. List a handful of item categories that appear in purchase events
SELECT DISTINCT it.item_category
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`,
     UNNEST(items) AS it
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Compute a per‑event tax rate (for quick inspection)
SELECT
  ecommerce.transaction_id,
  ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd / NULLIF(ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd,0) AS tax_rate
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Aggregate purchase revenue & tax by item category
SELECT
  it.item_category,
  SUM(ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd) AS total_tax_usd,
  SUM(ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd) AS total_rev_usd
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`,
     UNNEST(items) AS it
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
GROUP BY it.item_category
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Calculate the tax‑rate per category and sort descending
SELECT
  it.item_category,
  SUM(ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd) / NULLIF(SUM(ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd),0) AS tax_rate
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`,
     UNNEST(items) AS it
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
GROUP BY it.item_category
ORDER BY tax_rate DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Fetch the single highest‑tax‑rate category (top 1)
SELECT
  it.item_category,
  SUM(ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd) / NULLIF(SUM(ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd),0) AS tax_rate
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`,
     UNNEST(items) AS it
WHERE event_date = '20201130'
  AND event_name = 'purchase'
GROUP BY it.item_category
ORDER BY tax_rate DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 8. Pull transactions that belong to that top category
WITH top_cat AS (
  SELECT it.item_category
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`,
       UNNEST(items) AS it
  WHERE event_date = '20201130'
    AND event_name = 'purchase'
  GROUP BY it.item_category
  ORDER BY SUM(ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd)/NULLIF(SUM(ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd),0) DESC
  LIMIT 1
)
SELECT
  e.ecommerce.transaction_id,
  e.ecommerce.total_item_quantity,
  e.ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd,
  e.ecommerce.purchase_revenue
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130` AS e
JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS it
JOIN top_cat tc
ON it.item_category = tc.item_category
WHERE e.event_date = '20201130'
  AND e.event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same as #8 but also echo the category & event‑level tax‑rate
WITH top_cat AS (
  SELECT it.item_category
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130`,
       UNNEST(items) AS it
  WHERE event_date = '20201130'
    AND event_name = 'purchase'
  GROUP BY it.item_category
  ORDER BY SUM(ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd)/NULLIF(SUM(ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd),0) DESC
  LIMIT 1
)
SELECT
  it.item_category,
  e.ecommerce.transaction_id,
  e.ecommerce.total_item_quantity,
  e.ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd,
  e.ecommerce.tax_value_in_usd / NULLIF(e.ecommerce.purchase_revenue_in_usd,0) AS event_tax_rate
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201130` AS e
JOIN UNNEST(e.items) AS it
JOIN top_cat tc
ON it.item_category = tc.item_category
WHERE e.event_date = '20201130'
  AND e.event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL answer query – returns required fields for every purchase
--     in the highest‑tax‑rate item category on 30‑Nov‑2020
WITH top_category AS (
